{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-73680/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73680"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-73651/selection/7eca9791d106f9296c3d0f16e091c069df664b0f2935e2e770a63bbca2aa59de" :answer/id #uuid "028df1de-8591-5a11-9974-87c12d27c0b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73651/selection/ea8649c30be2aa5d312440adeeb5346c552be6eab9622637e79da38d6bb3f3ac" :answer/id #uuid "5a99db91-30a7-539c-a388-51f1ea62ce71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73651/selection/cc4f1a33fb2d2bde82c6d453a92b109b4bbdaa0a8787cbbb13af57823827f600" :answer/id #uuid "3304334f-8b4a-5903-8d3a-9fd522579dac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73651/selection/765b3c78db14fe9f27175f4dfe74a5f8ba31520751794253fc287df751a88ce9" :answer/id #uuid "2466f75a-6305-5af3-b476-957171788c60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73651/selection/c775d3ca39fc0f5bfa51c274141858ec27e3cf9fd92a0a3519118fb925ae8a7f" :answer/id #uuid "347e20c5-3736-5d9d-b23b-7d33652934bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73651/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc4336d-0944-5804-92f5-eddebf454871" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73651/selection/7eca9791d106f9296c3d0f16e091c069df664b0f2935e2e770a63bbca2aa59de" :answer-field/choices ["q-73651/selection/7eca9791d106f9296c3d0f16e091c069df664b0f2935e2e770a63bbca2aa59de" "q-73651/selection/ea8649c30be2aa5d312440adeeb5346c552be6eab9622637e79da38d6bb3f3ac" "q-73651/selection/cc4f1a33fb2d2bde82c6d453a92b109b4bbdaa0a8787cbbb13af57823827f600" "q-73651/selection/765b3c78db14fe9f27175f4dfe74a5f8ba31520751794253fc287df751a88ce9" "q-73651/selection/c775d3ca39fc0f5bfa51c274141858ec27e3cf9fd92a0a3519118fb925ae8a7f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73651" :question/id #uuid "901d7bdd-b877-50eb-b32d-27ea08b5103b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73651" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-73651/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73651"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4716"] :question/problem "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4718"] :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/65e27c97ac04ff5514c6e1be3b010e7a6efa464131bfbad03a569f85e728ac85.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/cd8839007e548fcd0858ebc42904d3659d639da834b8caa476c4765b5b1a6b69.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4719"] :question/problem "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/ad2295d047cfcd60a05c2b155e8c21c511d295e76b0ad4056cf136fe54c272a1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/299ce95f91a5eb4029b2a8512c0b45c24562fac91cc4c70a7e00ecf42c320099.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/a8c0d76a5f06d1077eb45b6af4761434164602747f54d9644646b654d319b373.png)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058013/assets/f16a0b0e573cf3a8022f9c62993364a0d708c3a0cff0b4bb4cb0eadde7848f56.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
